      'Suppose a worker issued IDs at T = 105 and its clock then reads T = 104. Issuing now could repeat any ID it created during millisecond 104. For a rollback this small, pause briefly until the clock passes 105. A pause does not suit a 20-second step, because every caller would stall for 20 seconds. Stop issuing and alert instead, or switch to a fresh worker identity under a carefully bounded policy. Twitter’s Snowflake took the strict route and refused to generate IDs until the clock passed its last issued time. The cost is availability: that worker serves nothing during the wait, so callers need other workers to turn to.',
