      'Follow a partition with example timings. Worker A holds ID 17 on a 30-second lease and last renewed at t = 0, when the network cuts it off. At t = 30 the registry considers the lease expired and could hand 17 to Worker B. If A keeps issuing, both can produce identical IDs. Fencing means stopping an expired holder from acting. A classic fencing token, a rising number that a storage server uses to reject old holders, does not fit here because generating an ID touches no such server. So the worker fences itself: it checks its lease timer before each ID and stops when the lease runs out. The registry waits an extra margin, perhaps 10 seconds, before reassigning, to cover clock drift and process pauses.',
